{# DECIDED: prometheus-callers sits in the observability namespace and admits callers from
   the homelab namespace and from flux-system, so it stays bespoke.
   The callers model (`netpol_from` / `netpol_fences`, macro in
   ansible/templates/networkpolicy.yml.j2) renders one TCP port admitting same-namespace `app:`
   peers, and its header rules out growing it to absorb another shape. Census: #3701.
#}
---
# prometheus is one of three observability workloads (with loki and alertmanager) that need a
# per-workload grant for an in-cluster, cross-namespace caller: monitor-bridge, homelab-mcp and homepage all run in
# {{ k8s_namespace }} and reach it over the network (PROMETHEUS_URL on monitor-bridge and on
# homelab-mcp, and the Headlamp tile's customapi widget URL on
# homepage — roles/k8s/homepage/templates/config/services.yaml.j2). A fourth cross-namespace caller
# runs in flux-system: the soak Job (D5.3, docs/target-architecture/deploy/d5-bulk-move.md,
# *Reaching Prometheus*). It reads M2's `homelab:pod_service:info` and the critical alerts to
# decide whether a promotion is healthy. Its name moves with each revision's SHA, so the peer
# selects the stable pod label `app: soak` instead of `job-name`. A fifth runs in
# {{ k8s_namespace }}: crowdsec's verify Job, `app: crowdsec-verify`
# (roles/k8s/crowdsec/templates/verify/job.yaml.j2), which reads the edge's answers to
# daniel-pi's probe from here. prometheus itself is ALSO read on host loopback
# by `otelq` (a workstation host process — see observability/CLAUDE.md), covered the same way as
# tempo/otel-collector below, not by this file. Headlamp's Prometheus plugin is a sixth
# in-cluster reader that this file also does NOT admit, on purpose: it queries through the API
# server's service proxy (roles/k8s/headlamp/templates/rbac.yaml.j2), so the hop that reaches
# the pod is the API server's, from the node's cni0/flannel.1 address — the same node-CIDR peer
# that carries otelq. The other four need no per-workload policy, each
# for a different reason — don't collapse them into one mechanism:
#   - tempo, otel-collector: loopback-hostPort callers only, admitted by the node-CIDR `ipBlock`
#     peer (`netpol_baseline_obs_node_cidrs`, networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2), which carries
#     no `ports` restriction — trimming that list to "nameable" entries breaks otelq silently.
#   - grafana: Traefik only, admitted by that same file's explicit traefik
#     namespaceSelector+podSelector peer — unrelated to the ipBlock/CIDR list above.
#   - kube-state-metrics: no external caller at all; prometheus scrapes it from inside the
#     namespace, covered by the plain `podSelector: {}` peer.
#
# INGRESS ONLY, same as the rest of this role: an Egress policy selects pods correctly on this
# cluster and blocks nothing (measured 2026-08-07). Writing one would read like a control in the
# repo and do nothing in the cluster.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: prometheus-callers
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: prometheus
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# `| bool`, matching the `when:` in tasks/main.yml — same trap netpol_baseline_obs_enforced
   already guards against for the namespace baseline: a STRING override like
   `-e netpol_baseline_obs_enforced=false` is truthy to bare Jinja and False to Ansible's
   `when:`, so without `| bool` the two sides disagree and this policy would fence prometheus
   with no probe to catch it. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_obs_enforced | bool %}
    - from:
        # namespaceSelector and podSelector are SIBLING KEYS in ONE list item, so they AND
        # together: one of the named apps, but only inside {{ k8s_namespace }}. As two
        # separate list items they would OR, admitting the entire {{ k8s_namespace }} namespace
        # (or any pod anywhere carrying one of these labels) rather than just these four
        # callers. The soak peer below follows the same rule.
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ k8s_namespace }}
          podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values: [monitor-bridge, homelab-mcp, homepage, crowdsec-verify]
        # The soak Job, as ONE item for the same reason: `app: soak`, but only inside
        # flux-system. Split in two, the namespaceSelector alone would admit every pod in
        # flux-system. D5.3b2's Job template sets the label on its POD template, because a
        # NetworkPolicy matches pod labels and never the Job object's own.
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: flux-system
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: soak
      # Port-9090-wide is the finest an L3/L4 NetworkPolicy can express — it does not
      # distinguish a read (query) from a write/admin call on the same port. That is only safe
      # because Prometheus runs today without --web.enable-admin-api, --web.enable-lifecycle or
      # --web.enable-remote-write-receiver (all three absent from observability/prometheus.yaml.j2
      # as of this writing). If any of those flags is ever added, this grant silently becomes a
      # write/admin grant to all five callers with no NetworkPolicy-side change to
      # flag in review — check for that when reviewing a future flag addition. homepage is the
      # caller that makes that check matter most: its widget proxy fetches whatever URL its
      # rendered config names, so its reach here is only ever as narrow as services.yaml.j2.
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 9090
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
